### Complexity

Let `m` be the number of rows and `n` the number of columns.

- Time: \\(O((mn)^2)\\) worst case. Each minute scans the whole grid, and infection can take up to \\(O(mn)\\) minutes to spread through a long path.
- Auxiliary space: \\(O(1)\\), because we use only a few variables and direction arrays.

Drawback: We repeatedly scan every cell, even when most cells are empty or already rotten. We can do better with BFS.

## Approach 2: Multi-source BFS using a queue and time — Your code (optimal)

This is the standard and most efficient solution for Rotting Oranges.

### Core idea

Instead of scanning the entire grid again and again, use a queue to process rotten oranges in the order they spread infection.

Why multi-source BFS?

Suppose the initial grid is:

```
2  1  2
1  1  1
1  1  1
```

There are two initially rotten oranges.

Both start spreading infection at minute `0`. If we start BFS from only one of them, we would not correctly account for simultaneous infection.

Therefore:

1. Insert all initially rotten oranges into the queue.
2. Give each of them an initial time of `0`.
3. Count all initially fresh oranges.
4. Process each rotten orange and infect its fresh neighbors.
5. Mark each newly infected orange immediately and add it to the queue with `time + 1`.
6. Track the maximum time.
7. Compare the number of initially fresh oranges with the number that became rotten.

This is called Multi-Source BFS because BFS starts simultaneously from multiple source cells.

### Your code

```
class Pair {
    int row;
    int col;
    int step;

    public Pair(int row, int col, int step) {
        this.row = row;
        this.col = col;
        this.step = step;
    }
}

class Solution {
    public int orangesRotting(int[][] grid) {
        int m = grid.length;
        int n = grid[0].length;

        int count = 0;
        Queue<Pair> q = new LinkedList<>();

        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
                if (grid[i][j] == 2) {
                    q.offer(new Pair(i, j, 0));
                } else if (grid[i][j] == 1) {
                    count++;
                }
            }
        }

        int cnt = 0;
        int time = 0;

        int nr[] = {-1, 0, 1, 0};
        int nc[] = {0, 1, 0, -1};

        while (!q.isEmpty()) {
            Pair p = q.poll();

            int r = p.row;
            int c = p.col;
            int s = p.step;

            time = Math.max(time, s);

            for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
                int rn = r + nr[i];
                int cn = c + nc[i];

                if (rn >= 0 && rn < m &&
                    cn >= 0 && cn < n &&
                    grid[rn][cn] == 1) {

                    q.offer(new Pair(rn, cn, s + 1));

                    grid[rn][cn] = 2;
                    cnt++;
                }
            }
        }

        if (count != cnt) {
            return -1;
        }

        return time;
    }
}
```

Your approach is correct and optimal. Let's understand every part.

