## Approach 3: Multi-source BFS using level-by-level traversal

Your solution stores the infection time inside every `Pair`:

```
new Pair(rn, cn, s + 1)
```

We can simplify this by processing the queue one level at a time.

Every BFS level represents one minute of infection.

### Idea

1. Insert all initially rotten oranges into the queue.
2. Count all initially fresh oranges.
3. Process exactly the number of elements currently in the queue.
4. Infect their fresh neighbors and enqueue them.
5. If at least one new orange became rotten in this level, increase the minute counter.
6. Continue until the queue is empty or all fresh oranges have rotted.

This removes the `step` variable from the `Pair` class.

### Java code

```
import java.util.*;

class Solution {
    public int orangesRotting(int[][] grid) {
        int m = grid.length;
        int n = grid[0].length;

        Queue<int[]> q = new ArrayDeque<>();
        int fresh = 0;

        // Add all initially rotten oranges
        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
                if (grid[i][j] == 2) {
                    q.offer(new int[]{i, j});
                } else if (grid[i][j] == 1) {
                    fresh++;
                }
            }
        }

        if (fresh == 0) {
            return 0;
        }

        int[] dr = {-1, 0, 1, 0};
        int[] dc = {0, 1, 0, -1};

        int minutes = 0;

        while (!q.isEmpty() && fresh > 0) {

            int size = q.size();
            boolean rotted = false;

            // Process only the current BFS level
            for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
                int[] curr = q.poll();

                int r = curr[0];
                int c = curr[1];

                for (int d = 0; d < 4; d++) {
                    int nr = r + dr[d];
                    int nc = c + dc[d];

                    if (nr >= 0 && nr < m &&
                        nc >= 0 && nc < n &&
                        grid[nr][nc] == 1) {

                        grid[nr][nc] = 2;
                        fresh--;
                        rotted = true;

                        q.offer(new int[]{nr, nc});
                    }
                }
            }

            // One minute passed if at least one orange rotted
            if (rotted) {
                minutes++;
            }
        }

        return fresh == 0 ? minutes : -1;
    }
}
```

### Important explanation

1\. Count fresh oranges and enqueue rotten oranges

```
if (grid[i][j] == 2) {
    q.offer(new int[]{i, j});
} else if (grid[i][j] == 1) {
    fresh++;
}
```

All initially rotten oranges become the starting BFS level.

2\. Capture the current level size

```
int size = q.size();
```

This is crucial.

Suppose the queue currently contains:

```
[(0,1), (1,0)]
```

These are the oranges that are already rotten at the beginning of this minute.

We process exactly these two oranges. Any newly rotten oranges are added to the queue but won't be processed until the next minute.

3\. Decrease the fresh count immediately

```
grid[nr][nc] = 2;
fresh--;
```

The newly infected orange is marked immediately so it cannot be enqueued by another rotten neighbor.

4\. Increase minutes only if infection spreads

```
if (rotted) {
    minutes++;
}
```

This avoids increasing the time for a final BFS level in which no fresh orange becomes rotten.

