## 7. Why BFS and not DFS?

This is a common interview question.

The problem asks for the minimum time needed for all oranges to become rotten.

Each movement from one cell to an adjacent cell takes exactly one minute.

BFS explores cells in increasing order of their distance from the initially rotten oranges:

```
Distance 0 → Initially rotten oranges
Distance 1 → Oranges reached after 1 minute
Distance 2 → Oranges reached after 2 minutes
Distance 3 → Oranges reached after 3 minutes
```

Because BFS visits cells in increasing distance order, it finds the earliest possible infection time for each reachable cell.

DFS does not guarantee this order. It might follow a long path first, even if a shorter infection path exists.

Therefore, multi-source BFS is the natural and optimal graph traversal for this problem.

